---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Sidebar from '../../components/Sidebar.astro';
import LoopCircle from '../../components/LoopCircle.astro';
import TraditionalLine from '../../components/TraditionalLine.astro';
import ObservabilityCompare from '../../components/ObservabilityCompare.astro';
import PerformanceCompare from '../../components/PerformanceCompare.astro';

type Shift = { practice: string; up: boolean; why: string };
type Phase = { stage: string; items: Shift[] };

const phases: Phase[] = [
  {
    stage: 'Specify',
    items: [
      {
        practice: 'Requirements',
        up: true,
        why: 'The spec is the loop\'s input. Intent has to be explicit and machine-readable, not held in someone\'s head.',
      },
      {
        practice: 'Modular architecture',
        up: true,
        why: 'Guard rails that scope the agent to the area of interest. Without them, the whole codebase is in play.',
      },
      {
        practice: 'Documentation',
        up: true,
        why: 'Context the agent reads. Stale docs become wrong changes, faster.',
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    stage: 'Implement',
    items: [
      {
        practice: 'Implementation',
        up: false,
        why: 'The agent writes the code. Producing each change is no longer the scarce work.',
      },
      {
        practice: 'Refactoring',
        up: false,
        why: 'The agent can rewrite. Humans decide whether the rewrite stayed in bounds.',
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    stage: 'Evaluate',
    items: [
      {
        practice: 'Unit tests',
        up: true,
        why: 'Fast, cheap rubric for local behaviour. The agent has to pass them on every loop.',
      },
      {
        practice: 'Integration tests',
        up: true,
        why: 'Prove the pieces still work together. Harder for the agent to satisfy by editing the test.',
      },
      {
        practice: 'Contract tests',
        up: true,
        why: 'Executable boundaries between modules. They keep the agent from breaking surfaces it was not allowed to change.',
      },
      {
        practice: 'End-to-end tests',
        up: true,
        why: 'Closest executable version of the spec. Did the user-facing behaviour actually happen?',
      },
      {
        practice: 'Performance tests',
        up: true,
        why: 'Profile the current code, then the new code, under the same tests. The delta is the evidence.',
      },
      {
        practice: 'Security tests',
        up: true,
        why: 'Permissions and invariants have to fail the loop, not wait for a human gate.',
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    stage: 'Ship',
    items: [
      {
        practice: 'Code review',
        up: false,
        why: 'Reading every generated line does not scale. Review the spec, the tests, and the boundaries.',
      },
      {
        practice: 'CI',
        up: true,
        why: 'Where the loop runs. Less a merge gate, more a place to try alternatives and keep the ones that pass.',
      },
      {
        practice: 'Preview environments',
        up: true,
        why: 'Where the agent refines a feature, with telemetry it can reach over a REST API or MCP.',
      },
      {
        practice: 'Deployment',
        up: true,
        why: 'Progressive rollout and automatic rollback are how you give the agent bounded autonomy.',
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    stage: 'Operate',
    items: [
      {
        practice: 'Telemetry',
        up: true,
        why: 'Triggers the next loop. Not a report someone reads later.',
      },
      {
        practice: 'Product metrics',
        up: true,
        why: 'Part of the rubric. They decide whether a change is kept, rolled back, or tried again.',
      },
      {
        practice: 'Incident recovery',
        up: true,
        why: 'The loop will ship more often. Rollback and recovery have to be automatic and tested.',
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Layout title="Self-Improving Software - John Oram">
  <Sidebar slot="sidebar" />

  <div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto">
    <div class="text-center my-8">
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Self-Improving Software</h1>
      <hr class="border-white/40" />
    </div>

    <p class="text-white/70 italic mb-2 leading-relaxed">
      Software development is going to move to an LLM loop that keeps running until the project meets the spec. Spec updates and telemetry are what trigger it to run again.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-3">The thesis</h2>
    <p class="text-white/80 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
      Today, humans convert intent into code. Next, an agent loops: implement, evaluate, ship, read telemetry, go again — until the software matches the spec. When the spec changes, or telemetry shows the software is off, the loop starts over.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-3">The traditional line</h2>
    <p class="text-white/80 mb-2 leading-relaxed">
      Traditional software is a line. You develop it, then you operate it.
    </p>
    <TraditionalLine
      id="traditional-loop"
      description="Traditional software as a line: requirements, implement, and tests as development; production and telemetry as operation."
      nodes={[
        { label: 'Requirements', group: 'development' },
        { label: 'Implement', group: 'development' },
        { label: 'Tests', group: 'development' },
        { label: 'Production', group: 'operation' },
        { label: 'Telemetry', group: 'operation' },
      ]}
    />
    <p class="text-white/80 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
      Requirements, implementation, and tests are development. Production and telemetry are operation. The line does not close. A human still has to interpret the telemetry and decide what happens next.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-3">The self-improving loop</h2>
    <p class="text-white/80 mb-2 leading-relaxed">
      A human writes the spec. An agent reads it and runs the cycle.
    </p>
    <LoopCircle
      id="self-improving-loop"
      description="A highlighted human writes the spec. An agent reads it and runs a cycle of implement, evaluate and tests, production, and telemetry."
      nodes={[
        { label: 'Implement' },
        { label: 'Evaluate / Tests' },
        { label: 'Production' },
        { label: 'Telemetry' },
      ]}
    />
    <p class="text-white/80 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
      Implement, evaluate against tests, ship, read telemetry, and go again. Spec updates and new telemetry are the triggers. The engineering work is writing the spec, and giving the agent a way to prove it has been met.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-3">Where priorities have shifted</h2>
    <p class="text-white/80 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
      The old practices are still there. What changed is which ones absorb the effort.
    </p>
    <div class="mb-4">
      {phases.map(({ stage, items }) => (
        <div class="mt-8 first:mt-0">
          <h3 class="text-[#e8a04a] text-xs font-bold tracking-[0.14em] uppercase mb-1">{stage}</h3>
          <div class="divide-y divide-white/10">
            {items.map(({ practice, up, why }) => (
              <div class="flex gap-3 py-3">
                <span
                  class:list={[
                    'w-5 shrink-0 font-bold text-lg leading-none mt-0.5',
                    up ? 'text-[#e8a04a]' : 'text-white/35',
                  ]}
                >
                  {up ? '+' : '−'}
                </span>
                <div>
                  <div class="font-semibold text-white">{practice}</div>
                  <p class="text-white/70 text-sm leading-relaxed mt-0.5 mb-0">{why}</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-3">Tests and telemetry are the rubric</h2>
    <p class="text-white/80 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
      Tests say whether a change is allowed. Telemetry says whether it actually worked. Together they are the rubric the agent is graded against — not a human reading the diff.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-3">Performance is a comparison</h2>
    <p class="text-white/80 mb-2 leading-relaxed">
      A correctness test passes or fails. Performance has to be compared.
    </p>
    <PerformanceCompare />
    <p class="text-white/80 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
      Exercise the current code with a fixed set of tests and capture a profile — CPU, allocations, queries, whatever the system can record. That profile is the baseline. Run the same tests against the new version and capture the same profile. Side by side, the two profiles are the performance change: what got slower, what got more expensive, what moved.
    </p>
    <p class="text-white/80 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
      The agent does not get a dashboard. It gets a delta it can read, and a rule for whether that delta is allowed. A change that is behaviourally correct and slower still fails evaluation, unless the spec said otherwise.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-3">Observability becomes an input</h2>
    <p class="text-white/80 mb-2 leading-relaxed">
      The difference is where telemetry goes.
    </p>
    <ObservabilityCompare />
    <p class="text-white/80 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
      In the line, telemetry goes to a human. In the loop, it goes back to the agent. Branch environments should carry their own telemetry so the agent can refine a feature before it ships — reachable over a REST API or MCP, not a dashboard a person has to click through.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-3">Modular architecture is the guard rail</h2>
    <p class="text-white/80 mb-8 leading-relaxed">
      Modular architecture scopes the agent to the area of interest. Guard rails define which parts of the system it may change, which contracts must stay stable, and how much damage a bad change can do. Without them, the loop has the whole codebase as its search space.
    </p>

    <div class="border-t border-white/10 pt-8 mt-8">
      <a href="/opinion/maintainable_code" class="inline-block text-white/40 hover:text-white/60 text-sm transition-colors no-underline">
        ← Opinion
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
